WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.880
Speech contains redundancy: overlapping information that helps a listener recover a message when some sound is missed.

00:00:07.550 --> 00:00:10.990
Syntax supplies grammatical expectations.

00:00:11.335 --> 00:00:13.975
Semantics supplies meaning relationships.

00:00:14.465 --> 00:00:17.825
Situation supplies the physical and social context.

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:25.240
A familiar topic can help, but a child still learning the language may not have the same predictions as an experienced adult.

00:00:26.085 --> 00:00:29.765
Noise in this lecture is broader than competing sound.

00:00:30.295 --> 00:00:38.135
Poor lighting, unclear articulation, unfamiliar vocabulary, and lack of topic knowledge can all reduce usable information.

00:00:38.945 --> 00:00:42.065
Finally, identify the demand of the listening task.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.480
Detection means noticing sound.

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.060
Discrimination means telling whether sounds differ.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:52.200
Identification means selecting or naming what was heard.

00:00:52.605 --> 00:00:54.925
Comprehension means understanding the message.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:01:00.470
A successful response at one level does not automatically prove success at the next.

